WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.610 --> 00:00:06.839
“Peggy I don’t have a shield.”

00:00:06.839 --> 00:00:07.839
“What?”

00:00:07.839 --> 00:00:11.670
“Yeah I don’t have a shield.”

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:15.480
“Where is it?

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:20.380
It’s right by the radiator.

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:21.380
It’s moving… maybe at half a foot a second.

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:24.210
It looks like straight down from the radiator
angle.”

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:28.609
“We copy and we see it.”

00:00:28.609 --> 00:00:35.059
While on a six-and-a-half hour spacewalk on
the International Space Station in 2017, astronauts

00:00:35.059 --> 00:00:39.970
Peggy Whitson and Shane Kimbrough experienced
a bit of clumsiness that would pose no problems

00:00:39.970 --> 00:00:43.860
on Earth, but can cause some major headaches
in space.

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:48.539
They dropped a piece of a debris shield that
was part of the docking port, a fabric covering

00:00:48.539 --> 00:00:56.190
that weighed 8 kilograms and measured 162
cm by 59cm–about the size of a car door.

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:59.420
And that debris shield had an important role
to play.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:04.260
It was supposed to cover a docking port on
the ship, protecting it from extreme temperature

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:06.869
changes and orbital debris.

00:01:06.869 --> 00:01:09.729
Something the ISS could not do without.

00:01:09.729 --> 00:01:15.740
The shield was a whipple shield, which uses
the hypervelocity of space debris to its advantage.

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:20.299
Using thin layers of shield to obliterate
the debris into tiny fragments that the inner

00:01:20.299 --> 00:01:26.439
shield can withstand as the energy of the
debris is spread out over a larger distance.

00:01:26.439 --> 00:01:29.210
Back on Earth, the Mission Control team got
to work.

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:33.960
Thanks to the fact that they have replicas
of everything in space, every nut and bolt,

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:35.799
and every piece of fabric.

00:01:35.799 --> 00:01:40.369
Mission control found a way for Whitson and
Kimbrough to use wire ties and a different

00:01:40.369 --> 00:01:45.450
piece of cloth as a substitute for the lost
whipple shield, until a replacement could

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:47.649
be sent up on another rocket.

00:01:47.649 --> 00:01:48.649
[1]

00:01:48.649 --> 00:01:51.521
Having dealt with that problem, there was
just one more thing to worry about: whether

00:01:51.521 --> 00:01:55.520
that lost piece of the shield would come slamming
back into the space station.

00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:00.060
The shield was monitored closely for two orbits,
before it was determined there was no risk

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:01.969
it would hit the ISS.

00:02:01.969 --> 00:02:06.079
Kimbrough and Whitson could breathe a sigh
of relief, and Whitson could properly celebrate

00:02:06.079 --> 00:02:09.020
having completed the most spacewalks of any
woman.

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:10.020
[2]

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:13.490
But just how common is it for astronauts to
lose their grip on tools and other objects

00:02:13.490 --> 00:02:15.980
while in the microgravity of space?

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:19.930
How costly are these brief bouts of “butter
fingers” on the ISS?

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:25.040
And what happens if the newly-made space debris
is on a collision course with the station

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:27.290
or another satellite?

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:32.379
These are all scenarios NASA and the astronauts
themselves have to prepare for.

00:02:32.379 --> 00:02:35.750
Like with anything else, sometimes accidents
happen.

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:40.310
The lost shield wasn’t the first time astronauts
have lost something to the swirl of space

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:44.140
while conducting an extravehicular activity,
or spacewalk.

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:50.019
In 2006, astronaut Piers Seller dropped a
spatula while doing work on the space station—and

00:02:50.019 --> 00:02:54.940
apparently had a lot of spatulas waiting for
him in his office when he came back to Earth

00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:56.010
after the mission.

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:59.159
[3] That same year, another astronaut lost
a camera.

00:02:59.159 --> 00:03:04.370
And in 2008, another astronaut was dealing
with a grease gun explosion when she lost

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:05.370
a tool bag.

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:06.370
“Oh great.

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:10.940
Um, we have a lost tool.”

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:15.120
Part of the reason items can become lost is
because of the challenge of maneuvering small

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:18.270
objects while wearing massive gloves.

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:24.489
Astronaut Drew Feustel has gone to space three
times for a total of 226 days, and conducted

00:03:24.489 --> 00:03:30.230
nine spacewalks, accumulating the second-longest
amount of EVA time among US astronauts.

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:36.650
He’s plenty familiar with the difficulty
of doing work in a bulky 160 kilogram spacesuit.

00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:43.380
I equate it to wearing a set of mechanics
gloves with welding gloves on top, that’s

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:47.730
how it feels getting tactile feedback with
your fingertips and hands.

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:53.489
There’s not really a good way to train that
except expose yourself to that environment.

00:03:53.489 --> 00:03:58.150
And the way NASA has astronauts practice in
advance of going to space is by wearing their

00:03:58.150 --> 00:04:04.640
suits in a massive pool, which has a precise
recreation of the ISS built underwater for

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:06.650
the astronauts to practice on.

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:10.951
They learn how to maneuver in their suits,
how to manipulate tools for repair work, how

00:04:10.951 --> 00:04:15.700
to move objects from one area to another,
and how to perform an emergency rescue on

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:18.630
team members–all while still on the planet.

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:23.330
It’s a pretty good analog,, but not everyone
gets as much time to practice as they might

00:04:23.330 --> 00:04:24.360
like.

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:30.770
Historically, during the shuttle area, we’d
train at a 10 to one ratio, meaning we’d

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:38.410
practice a technique or task ten times underwater
for every single time we saw in space, so

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:43.729
for every hour in space we’d have about
ten hours in the pool underwater training

00:04:43.729 --> 00:04:49.479
that task and that allows you to really build
the knowledge for what you can expect the

00:04:49.479 --> 00:04:54.250
feedback to be through your hands as you’re
working with the objects in space.

00:04:54.250 --> 00:05:01.170
We see much less of that now, we’re probably
at a two to three to one ratio if that, so

00:05:01.170 --> 00:05:06.120
it's more difficult to have that exposure
and it becomes a challenge.

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:10.540
The astronauts also have a number of safeguards
for themselves and all their hardware once

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:13.220
they’re outside the station doing an EVA.

00:05:13.220 --> 00:05:18.370
They’re always attached to small cable tethers
on retractable reels that are either 17 metres

00:05:18.370 --> 00:05:23.330
or 26 metres long, which will self-retract,
allowing the astronauts to move around without

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:25.139
fear of drifting too far away.

00:05:25.139 --> 00:05:30.130
On top of that, they’ll have small local
tethers that only extend about 1.5 metres,

00:05:30.130 --> 00:05:34.660
which keep them close enough to the station
that they can use both hands for a specific

00:05:34.660 --> 00:05:38.240
activity, but they still have a little more
room to maneuver.

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:43.330
Then there are all the tethers for the tools,
bags, and whatever other gear they might be

00:05:43.330 --> 00:05:44.330
carrying.

00:05:44.330 --> 00:05:50.720
we always attempt to make sure that every
single item that goes outside the airlock

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:57.620
with us has some attachment to something,
and that’s achieved through those tethers.

00:05:57.620 --> 00:06:04.240
We are not always successful in keeping those
tethers attached, and it’s always a little

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:08.500
bit scary when you find yourself holding an
object and you realize that there’s no tether

00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:15.120
attached to it either because it was bumped
off or came off accidentally or in some cases

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:16.910
it was never attached in the first place…

00:06:16.910 --> 00:06:21.570
I’ve had a couple experiences with holding
items that didn’t have tethers on them and

00:06:21.570 --> 00:06:26.710
expected to have tethers on them, that’s
where you reach down carefully and find a

00:06:26.710 --> 00:06:29.880
tether somewhere anywhere to connect back
to that thing.

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:36.229
But on one particular occasion, during his
ninth spacewalk in March 2018, Feustel lost

00:06:36.229 --> 00:06:37.310
hold of an object.

00:06:37.310 --> 00:06:41.860
He’d been working to install cameras and
wireless communication equipment next to a

00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:47.389
docking port, as well as securing a loose
component on a solar array blanket box when

00:06:47.389 --> 00:06:52.080
he lost a wire tie—what he describes as
something like a big garbage tie.

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:56.259
When he’d pulled the tether to which the
wire tie was attached, it turned out that

00:06:56.259 --> 00:07:00.160
the jaws of it were still open, and the tie
quickly floated away.

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:05.280
There have been crew members in the past who
have lunged after objects, you could maybe

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:08.760
do that if you have a short tether on, you’re
not gonna lunge very far, if you have a long

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:13.580
tether on and you lunge you’re gonna travel
for another 55 or 85 ft before that tether

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:17.550
hopefully stops you from your motion hand
you may or may not have been successful at

00:07:17.550 --> 00:07:20.569
reaching the object that is now floating away
from you.

00:07:20.569 --> 00:07:25.789
but in my case i was attached to a foot restraint,
which m eans my feet were fixed, there was

00:07:25.789 --> 00:07:32.449
really no way for me to move and I simp\ly
watched this object float away from me.

00:07:32.449 --> 00:07:37.970
at that time all you can really do is call
Mission Control and say I’ve lost an object,

00:07:37.970 --> 00:07:44.160
this is what it was, and this is how large
it is, and this is the direction it’s traveling,

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:49.879
and this is how fast I think it’s going
and that data gets reported to our tracking

00:07:49.879 --> 00:08:01.509
officers at mission control and that gets
reported pretty much around the entire space

00:08:01.509 --> 00:08:06.980
debris network, which exists not only in the
US but internationally to track items that

00:08:06.980 --> 00:08:12.120
are floating in space that are not really
controlled in any way.

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:17.509
Just as with the piece of dropped shield in
2017, NASA had to closely monitor the trajectory

00:08:17.509 --> 00:08:22.250
of the little wire tie—and in this case,
despite the object being much smaller, it

00:08:22.250 --> 00:08:24.320
ended up being more of a problem.

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:31.490
I was told after the loss of my wire tie was
for the first time in history a satellite

00:08:31.490 --> 00:08:37.409
had to change its orbital altitude because
of a free floating object in space, for fear

00:08:37.409 --> 00:08:42.700
of damage to the satellite, and that object
was my wire tie that I had lost, was floating

00:08:42.700 --> 00:08:49.870
around and chad an impending collision with
some type of satellite that was important

00:08:49.870 --> 00:08:54.800
to somebody and they actually had to change
the orbital altitude of that satellite in

00:08:54.800 --> 00:09:01.269
order to create a greater distance or miss
distance between those two objects.

00:09:01.269 --> 00:09:09.620
Something as small as a wire tie could destroy
an entire module on the space station or even

00:09:09.620 --> 00:09:13.850
cause catastrophic damage to any piece of
equipment in space.

00:09:13.850 --> 00:09:18.550
Of course, this issue with debris goes far
beyond a few mishaps with astronauts losing

00:09:18.550 --> 00:09:21.029
hold of things while on an EVA.

00:09:21.029 --> 00:09:25.930
NASA has a pretty good track record—on the
vast majority of spacewalks, everything that’s

00:09:25.930 --> 00:09:28.300
carried out is carried back inside.

00:09:28.300 --> 00:09:33.480
The real problem is that debris has been accumulating
in orbit around Earth ever since the Space

00:09:33.480 --> 00:09:37.779
Race began with the launch of Sputnik in 1957.

00:09:37.779 --> 00:09:43.540
By 1979, debris from satellites and failed
rockets was already posing enough of a problem

00:09:43.540 --> 00:09:48.500
that NASA created the Orbital Debris Program
to monitor the accumulating number of small

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:51.330
objects that could cause damage to spacecraft.

00:09:51.330 --> 00:09:52.330
[4]

00:09:52.330 --> 00:09:56.839
This program uses a network of ground based
and space based observation techniques.

00:09:56.839 --> 00:10:01.399
Like the USAF Space Surveillance network,
which can keep track of larger objects in

00:10:01.399 --> 00:10:02.399
space.

00:10:02.399 --> 00:10:07.579
While ground based radar like the Goldstone
Solar System Radar can detect much smaller

00:10:07.579 --> 00:10:08.579
objects.

00:10:08.579 --> 00:10:11.920
Of course it would be impossible to keep track
of all the debris.

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:18.250
Today there are about 23,000 objects larger
than a softball orbiting Earth, half-a-million

00:10:18.250 --> 00:10:23.610
1-cm-sized fragments, and about 100 million
micro-fragments that are one millimeter or

00:10:23.610 --> 00:10:24.660
slightly larger.

00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:29.021
[5] So computer models have been developed
to log and track space debris, which also

00:10:29.021 --> 00:10:33.290
alert controllers when a collision becomes
a threat and allows the avoidance maneuvers

00:10:33.290 --> 00:10:35.070
to be performed.

00:10:35.070 --> 00:10:40.830
The ISS has a risk management system when
a potential collision is detected.

00:10:40.830 --> 00:10:43.930
The ISS has an imaginary box drawn around
it.

00:10:43.930 --> 00:10:48.089
50 kilometres squared and point seven five
kilometres deep.

00:10:48.089 --> 00:10:52.959
This acts as an exclusion zone and any tracked
debris that could pass through it will send

00:10:52.959 --> 00:10:55.360
an alert to mission control.

00:10:55.360 --> 00:10:58.160
From there careful risk analysis begins.

00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:02.920
If there is a one in ten thousand to one in
one hundred thousand chance of collision the

00:11:02.920 --> 00:11:05.110
ISS receives a yellow warning.

00:11:05.110 --> 00:11:10.300
Which means flight controllers must perform
avoidance maneuvers if they do not interfere

00:11:10.300 --> 00:11:11.750
with mission objectives.

00:11:11.750 --> 00:11:16.550
This can be as simple as interfering with
microgravity experiments.

00:11:16.550 --> 00:11:21.180
If there is a greater than one in ten thousand
risk [6], then a red warning is received and

00:11:21.180 --> 00:11:26.770
the international space station must take
action, regardless of mission objectives.

00:11:26.770 --> 00:11:30.810
These exclusion zones exist for all satellites
in NASA’s database.

00:11:30.810 --> 00:11:37.170
For example on March 29th 2012 an automated
email alert was sent to the Fermi Gamma-Ray

00:11:37.170 --> 00:11:38.940
Space Telescope team.

00:11:38.940 --> 00:11:45.850
Informing them of a predicted incursion between
Fermi and Cosmos 1805, a retired Russian spy

00:11:45.850 --> 00:11:49.740
satellite, where the two would pass within
200 metres of each other.

00:11:49.740 --> 00:11:54.100
Forcing them to abandon their mission temporarily
and point their thrusters, which were designed

00:11:54.100 --> 00:11:59.230
to deorbit the satellite in their direction
of travel, performing a 1 second burn to prevent

00:11:59.230 --> 00:12:00.650
a potential collision.

00:12:00.650 --> 00:12:01.650
[7]

00:12:01.650 --> 00:12:04.839
Even the very smallest bits of debris, like
a piece of flaking paint, travel at speeds

00:12:04.839 --> 00:12:10.990
fast enough to do serious damage if they hit
a rocket or the ISS, and tracking debris this

00:12:10.990 --> 00:12:14.850
small is impossible, which is why we need
debris shields.

00:12:14.850 --> 00:12:19.910
Like the one dropped 2017, which ironically
became debris itself.

00:12:19.910 --> 00:12:24.930
However the debris shield was a relatively
large and light object, helping it deorbit

00:12:24.930 --> 00:12:29.180
quicker as atmospheric drag still exists at
this altitude.

00:12:29.180 --> 00:12:33.899
Forcing the ISS to perform regular boost burns
to maintain its orbit.

00:12:33.899 --> 00:12:39.350
Feustel says it’s also much harder to lose
heavier objects while working outside the

00:12:39.350 --> 00:12:40.380
space station.

00:12:40.380 --> 00:12:44.550
You know for a large object that weighs 800
lbs, if you let go of it with no tether on

00:12:44.550 --> 00:12:46.339
it, it’s not gonna go anywhere.

00:12:46.339 --> 00:12:48.980
Even if you bumped it with your hand it’s
not gonna go anywhere because you can’t

00:12:48.980 --> 00:12:51.610
put enough force into it to actually move
it.

00:12:51.610 --> 00:12:56.089
But something like a wire tie that’s just
a small metal wire, that’s super easy, if

00:12:56.089 --> 00:13:00.480
you just flick it with your fingertips or
bump it, that thing is gonna go cruising away

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:02.730
much faster than you can reach it.

00:13:02.730 --> 00:13:07.810
But it’s not only the risk of a collision
that causes problems when an astronaut loses

00:13:07.810 --> 00:13:09.110
something to space.

00:13:09.110 --> 00:13:14.330
There’s also the issue of whether or not
that tool had replacements on the space station.

00:13:14.330 --> 00:13:16.380
Consider the lost shield again.

00:13:16.380 --> 00:13:20.570
Something that weighs 8 kilograms doesn’t
sound very large, but when you factor in the

00:13:20.570 --> 00:13:23.970
cost of rocket launches, every kilo counts.

00:13:23.970 --> 00:13:28.670
At least in the case of the wire tie Feustel
lost, there were others available on the space

00:13:28.670 --> 00:13:29.670
station.

00:13:29.670 --> 00:13:34.000
And ultimately, the rogue wire tie didn’t
cause any damage—though Feustel says it

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:37.139
can be a little blow to an astronaut’s perfect
record.

00:13:37.139 --> 00:13:40.810
You lose style points for losing things in
space.

00:13:40.810 --> 00:13:46.220
It’s probably possible to find this out,
to do a web search and find out when I lost

00:13:46.220 --> 00:13:50.310
my wire tie, I remember saying something like,
well, I’m going to have to live with this

00:13:50.310 --> 00:13:55.110
for the rest of my life knowing I dropped
something in space, because up until that

00:13:55.110 --> 00:13:57.160
point I had a pretty good record.

00:13:57.160 --> 00:14:05.100
I had gone without dropping anything at all,
this last act of misfortune was my contribution

00:14:05.100 --> 00:14:07.900
to items floating in space that shouldn’t
be.

00:14:07.900 --> 00:14:10.149
So it’s more of a style thing.

00:14:10.149 --> 00:14:15.949
Nobody looks, it doesn’t reflect negatively,
i don’t think, on your spacewalking career,

00:14:15.949 --> 00:14:19.980
but it certainly is something that we all
strive not to do, and you don’t want to

00:14:19.980 --> 00:14:21.980
be the person that’s constantly losing things.

00:14:21.980 --> 00:14:26.090
In fact if you were somebody who constantly
lost things both in training and in space

00:14:26.090 --> 00:14:30.490
you probably wouldn’t be doing spacewalks
out on orbit.

00:14:30.490 --> 00:14:35.160
Dropping a tool in space is not something
any of us have to worry about on a daily basis,

00:14:35.160 --> 00:14:39.410
but having the right tool for the job and
knowing how to use it can make or break a

00:14:39.410 --> 00:14:40.410
project.

00:14:40.410 --> 00:14:45.000
Keysight, put the very best tools into engineers
hands, and are holding a virtual event for

